\id COL - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Colossians
\toc1 The Letter of Paul to the Assembly in Colossae
\toc2 Colossians
\toc3 Col
\mt1 Colossians

\imt Introduction
\ip This letter comes from Paul, in chains at the time of writing (4:3, 18), together with Timothy, to a small assembly of believers in Colossae — a town in the Lycus valley of Phrygia, in the Roman province of Asia, in what is now southwestern Turkey. Colossae sat about ten miles from Laodicea, with Hierapolis close by; all three towns are named in the letter, and all three had communities in view. The date is somewhere in the middle of the first century.
\ip Paul had never been there. He says so outright: these are people who have never seen his face (2:1). The community was founded by Epaphras, a man from Colossae itself, who carried the good news home and has now come to Paul with a report — their trust, their love, and something troubling.
\ip The trouble was a teaching being pressed on them, and its shape can be read off what Paul answers. It involved rules about food and drink, and the keeping of festivals, new moons, and sabbaths (2:16). It involved self-abasement and the worship of angels, and a man who elaborated on visions he claimed to have had (2:18). It laid down prohibitions — do not handle, do not taste, do not touch (2:21) — and hard treatment of the body (2:23). And it spoke of "the elemental things of the world," the powers thought to govern the created order (2:8, 20). Whatever the mixture was, the claim underneath it was always the same: what you have in the Messiah is not yet enough.
\ip Paul does not take it apart point by point. He answers it by saying who the Messiah is and what has already been done in him — everything God is dwells in him bodily, and those who are in him are already complete (1:15-20; 2:9-10). Chapters 1 and 2 establish that; chapters 3 and 4 draw out the way of living that follows from it, in the community, in the household, and toward outsiders.

\c 1
\s1 Greeting
\p
\v 1 Paul, one sent\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl apostolos\tl*: an emissary who carries the full authority of the one who sent him.\f* of the Christ Jesus through the will of God, and Timothy the brother,
\v 2 to the holy ones\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Greek \tl hagioi\tl*, "set-apart ones" — Israel's own covenant word for itself (Deuteronomy 7:6; Daniel 7:18). It is used here of people in a Phrygian market town who had never been part of Israel at all.\f* in Colossae, faithful brothers in the Christ: favor to you and peace from God our Father.
\s1 Thanks for What They Already Have
\p
\v 3 We give thanks to God, the Father of our Lord Jesus the Christ, always praying for you,
\v 4 having heard\f + \fr 1:4 \ft Paul knows this community only by report. He has never been to Colossae (2:1); what he knows, Epaphras told him (verse 8).\f* of your faithfulness in the Christ Jesus and of the love you have toward all the holy ones,
\v 5 because of the hope laid up for you in the heavens \add [i.e. stored and waiting, out of anyone's reach]\add*, which you heard of before \add [i.e. long before any of this new teaching reached you]\add* in the word of the truth, the good news
\v 6 that has come to you. As in all the world it is bearing fruit and growing\f + \fr 1:6 \ft The two verbs are the ones spoken over creation itself: "be fruitful and multiply" (Genesis 1:28 LXX). The good news is being described as a living thing doing what living things do.\f*, so it has been among you from the day you heard it and came to know the favor of God in truth,
\v 7 just as you learned it from Epaphras \add [i.e. the man from your own town who brought it to you in the first place]\add*, our beloved fellow slave, who is a faithful servant of the Christ on your behalf,
\v 8 and who made your love in the Spirit clear to us.
\s1 A Prayer for Full Knowing and Endurance
\p
\v 9 For this reason we too, from the day we heard it, have not stopped praying for you and asking that you may be filled with the knowing\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Greek \tl epignōsis\tl*, a strengthened form of the ordinary word for knowledge: not acquaintance with facts but thorough, first-hand knowing. It runs through the whole letter (1:10; 2:2; 3:10) and is what Paul asks for in place of what the other teaching offers.\f* of his will in all wisdom and spiritual understanding,
\v 10 so as to walk\f + \fr 1:10 \ft "Walk" is the Hebrew way of speaking of how a person conducts a whole life (\tl halak\tl*), not of a manner of moving. The letter uses it again at 2:6, 3:7, and 4:5.\f* worthily of the Lord, pleasing him in every way, bearing fruit in every good work and growing in the knowing of God,
\v 11 made strong with all strength according to the might of his glory, for all endurance and long-suffering with joy \add [— strength given not to lift you out of the pressure but to hold you steady inside it]\add*,
\v 12 giving thanks to the Father, who made you fit for the share in the allotment\f + \fr 1:12 \ft Greek \tl klēros\tl*, the word used for the portions of the promised land parcelled out among the tribes (Numbers 26:55-56; Joshua 14:2). Each tribe had its assigned share; Israel itself was the LORD's (Deuteronomy 32:9). The inheritance language is being transferred from territory to a domain.\f* of the holy ones in the light \add [i.e. the domain where God's light rules, not the dark]\add*.
\v 13 He rescued us\f + \fr 1:13 \ft The verb is the standard one for God's deliverance of Israel out of Egypt (Exodus 6:6; 14:30 LXX). What is being described is an exodus.\f* out of the authority of the darkness and moved us over into the kingdom \add [i.e. deported us out of one ruler's territory and resettled us in another's — the thing empires did to conquered peoples, run backwards]\add* of the Son of his love \add [i.e. his own beloved Son]\add*,
\v 14 in whom we have the ransoming\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Greek \tl apolytrōsis\tl*, the buying-out of a slave, and in Israel's scriptures the word for God's redemption of his people from Egypt (Deuteronomy 7:8). "Sending away" is \tl aphesis\tl*, used both for the forgiving of sin and for the release of debts in the jubilee year (Leviticus 25:10).\f*, the sending away of the sins.
\s1 Who the Son Is: First in Creation, First in Reconciling
\p
\v 15 He is the image\f + \fr 1:15 \ft Greek \tl eikōn\tl*, the word of Genesis 1:26-27, where humanity is made "according to our image." An image is not a lesser copy; it is what makes the unseen one visible. No one can see God and live (Exodus 33:20) — and here he is, seen.\f* of the unseen God, the firstborn of all creation\f + \fr 1:15 \ft "Firstborn" (\tl prōtotokos\tl*) is a title of rank, not a statement of sequence. God calls David "firstborn, highest of the kings of the earth" (Psalm 89:27), though David was the youngest of eight; he calls Israel "my firstborn son" (Exodus 4:22), though Israel was not the first nation to exist. The word says: the heir, the one who holds first place.\f*,
\v 16 because in him all things were created — in the heavens and upon the earth, the seen and the unseen, whether thrones or lordships or rulers or authorities \add [i.e. every rank of power there is, named or unnamed — and every one of them his creature]\add*. All things have been created through him and for him.
\v 17 And he himself is before all things, and in him all things hold together\f + \fr 1:17 \ft What Israel's scriptures said of God's own wisdom and word — that it holds all things together (Wisdom 1:7; Sirach 43:26) — is said here of him.\f*.
\v 18 And he himself is the head of the body, the assembly\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Greek \tl ekklēsia\tl*, the word the Greek scriptures use for the assembly of Israel gathered before God (Deuteronomy 4:10; 1 Kings 8:14). Not a new institution but that assembly, gathered now around the Messiah.\f*. He is the beginning, the firstborn from among the dead \add [i.e. first up out of death, and the rest come out behind him]\add*, so that in all things he himself might hold first place,
\v 19 because in him all the fullness\f + \fr 1:19 \ft Greek \tl plērōma\tl*, the sum total, the whole of what fills a thing. Stated outright at 2:9: all the fullness of what God is. The verb "dwell" (\tl katoikeō\tl*) is the temple word — God settling permanently in a place, not visiting it (1 Kings 8:27; Psalm 68:16).\f* was pleased to dwell,
\v 20 and through him to reconcile all things to himself, having made peace through the blood of his cross \add [— an execution stake, and that is where the peace was made]\add* — through him, whether the things upon the earth or the things in the heavens.
\s1 And This Reached You — If You Stay There
\p
\v 21 And you, who were once strangers and enemies in your thinking, in your evil works —
\v 22 he has now reconciled you in the body of his flesh through death \add [i.e. in a real body, by a real death — nothing about this was carried out at a distance from matter]\add*, to present you holy and without blemish and beyond accusation\f + \fr 1:22 \ft "Without blemish" (\tl amōmos\tl*) is the word for a sacrificial animal with no defect in it, the only kind that could be brought (Leviticus 1:3). "Beyond accusation" (\tl anenklētos\tl*) is a courtroom word: no charge can be laid.\f* before his face,
\v 23 if indeed you remain in the faith \add [i.e. provided you do in fact stay there]\add*, founded and firmly seated and not being shifted away \add [— and someone is working to shift you]\add* from the hope of the good news that you heard, the good news proclaimed in all creation under heaven \add [i.e. everywhere — what you were given is not some local version]\add*, of which I, Paul, became a servant.
\s1 My Own Work: Suffering, and a Secret Now Told Out
\p
\v 24 Now I rejoice in the sufferings on your behalf, and in my flesh I fill up in my turn what is still lacking of the afflictions of the Christ\f + \fr 1:24 \ft Not that the Messiah's death fell short of anything — Paul has just said all things were reconciled by it (verse 20). "Afflictions" (\tl thlipseis\tl*) is his word for the pressure and hardship of the present age, and Jewish expectation held that a set measure of such affliction had to be filled up before the end came (Daniel 12:1). His own beatings and chains are going into that measure.\f*, on behalf of his body, which is the assembly.
\v 25 I became its servant according to the stewardship of God given to me \add [i.e. a household put in my charge to manage, not a post I took up for myself]\add* for you, to fill out the word of God:
\v 26 the secret\f + \fr 1:26 \ft Greek \tl mystērion\tl*: not a riddle for the clever to solve, and not knowledge reserved for initiates, but a plan God kept hidden and has now announced in the open (Daniel 2:28 LXX). Its content is given outright in the next verse.\f* hidden away from the ages and from the generations, but now brought into the open to his holy ones.
\v 27 To them God willed to make known what is the wealth of the glory of this secret among the nations, which is: the Christ in you\f + \fr 1:27 \ft The Greek reads either "in you" or "among you." Standing where it does, right after "among the nations," the thing being called staggering is that Israel's Messiah is now present among Gentile peoples at all.\f*, the hope of the glory.
\v 28 Him we announce, warning every person and teaching every person in all wisdom, so that we may present every person \add [— every one of them; there is no inner circle here getting something the rest do not]\add* complete \add [i.e. brought to what he was made for, with nothing missing]\add* in the Christ.
\v 29 For this I toil, contending according to his working, which works in me with power.

\c 2
\s1 The Fight I Am Having for People I Have Never Met
\p
\v 1 For I want you to know how great a contest \add [i.e. what a fight this is]\add* I have for you and for those in Laodicea and for all who have not seen my face in the flesh,
\v 2 that their hearts may be strengthened, knit together in love, reaching all the wealth of full assurance of understanding, reaching a knowing of the secret of God — the Christ —
\v 3 in whom all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge lie hidden \add [i.e. stored there, the whole store of them — there is no reserve of wisdom kept anywhere else]\add*.
\v 4 I say this so that no one may reason you out of it with persuasive talk \add [i.e. with arguments that sound entirely reasonable]\add*.
\v 5 For though I am away in the flesh, yet in the spirit I am with you, and I rejoice to see your ranks in order and the solid front of your faith\f + \fr 2:5 \ft Both words are military. \tl Taxis\tl* is troops holding their formation; \tl stereōma\tl* is a line that does not give way. He is looking at them the way a commander looks at a front that is still holding.\f* toward the Christ.
\s1 Go On As You Began
\p
\v 6 So then, as you received\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Greek \tl parelabete\tl*, the technical word for taking over a tradition handed on intact — the same word used of receiving and passing on the accounts of the Lord's supper and the resurrection (1 Corinthians 11:23; 15:3). What was handed to them was not a set of principles but a person.\f* the Christ Jesus the Lord, walk in him,
\v 7 rooted and being built up in him, and made firm in the faith just as you were taught \add [i.e. by the standard of what you were told at the start, not of what is being added to it now]\add*, overflowing with thanksgiving.
\s1 Watch Out for the Philosophy — Everything God Is, Is in Him
\p
\v 8 Watch out that no one carries you off as plunder \add [i.e. hauls you away as spoil, the way a raiding party carries off captives]\add* through the philosophy\f + \fr 2:8 \ft The Greek has the definite article — "the philosophy." Not philosophy in general, but the particular teaching circulating at Colossae.\f* and empty deceit, according to the tradition of men, according to the elemental things of the world\f + \fr 2:8 \ft Greek \tl stoicheia tou kosmou\tl*. The word covered the basic components of the physical world, the rudiments of any discipline, and the heavenly bodies, which ancient cosmology took to be governed by spiritual powers. Paul's other use of it ties it to enslavement, to gods that are not gods, and to the keeping of "days and months and seasons and years" (Galatians 4:3, 8-10). Whatever precisely the Colossian teaching meant by it, he sets it against the Messiah as a rival standard.\f* and not according to the Christ.
\v 9 For in him all the fullness of what God is dwells bodily \add [i.e. in a body — not in some realm you have to leave your body behind to reach]\add*,
\v 10 and you have been filled full in him \add [i.e. you are already complete; there is nothing left to add to you]\add*, who is the head of every ruler and authority \add [— the powers this teaching wants you to reckon with answer to him]\add*.
\s1 What Was Already Done to You in Him
\p
\v 11 In him you were also circumcised with a circumcision not made by hands\f + \fr 2:11 \ft "Not made by hands" (\tl acheiropoiētos\tl*) marks a thing as God's work rather than human work; the same word is used of the temple not made by hands (Mark 14:58). Circumcision was the covenant sign cut in the flesh (Genesis 17:10-14). The claim here is that these Gentiles have already been marked as covenant people, and not with a knife.\f*, in the stripping off of the body of the flesh \add [i.e. the whole old self taken off, not a token piece of it]\add*, in the circumcision of the Christ,
\v 12 buried together with him in the immersion\f + \fr 2:12 \ft Going down under the water and coming up out of it enacts the burial and the rising (Romans 6:3-4).\f*, in which you were also raised together with him through faith in the working of God, who raised him from among the dead.
\v 13 And you, who were dead in the missteps and in the uncircumcision of your flesh \add [i.e. Gentiles, outside the covenant and carrying the mark of being outside it]\add* — he made you alive together with him, having freely forgiven us\f + \fr 2:13 \ft The pronoun turns here from "you" to "us." Having addressed the Colossians' own past, Paul folds himself and every believer into the forgiveness.\f* all the missteps,
\v 14 having wiped out the handwritten record of debt\f + \fr 2:14 \ft Greek \tl cheirographon\tl*, the ordinary business word for an IOU — a note of debt written out in the debtor's own hand, which is what made it binding.\f* that stood against us with its decrees. It was set over against us, and he has taken it out of the way, nailing it to the cross \add [— posted up on the stake, the way the charge against a condemned man was posted over his head, and cancelled there]\add*.
\v 15 He stripped the rulers and the authorities, put them on open display, and led them in triumph\f + \fr 2:15 \ft The image is the Roman triumph: a victorious general parading through the city with the conquered kings walking in chains behind his chariot. The cross was Rome's instrument for shaming the powerless — and it is named here as the procession in which the powers themselves were led as captives.\f* in him.
\s1 So Let No One Judge You or Rule You Out
\p
\v 16 So let no one judge you in eating and in drinking, or in the matter of a feast or a new moon or sabbaths\f + \fr 2:16 \ft Annual, monthly, weekly: the three together are the standard way Israel's scriptures name the whole sacred calendar (1 Chronicles 23:31; 2 Chronicles 31:3; Hosea 2:11). Naming all three covers the entire system at once.\f*.
\v 17 These are a shadow \add [i.e. the shape a thing throws ahead of itself on the ground]\add* of the things coming, but the body \add [i.e. the solid thing that cast it]\add* is the Christ's.
\v 18 Let no one rule you out of your prize \add [i.e. stand over you as umpire and call you disqualified]\add*, delighting in lowliness of mind\f + \fr 2:18 \ft The word is a good one elsewhere in this letter (3:12), but here it names a cultivated self-abasement that was part of the program — deliberate self-denial worn where it could be seen.\f* and the worship of the angels\f + \fr 2:18 \ft The Greek can mean worship directed toward angels, or the worship that the angels themselves perform, which the practitioner claims to have ascended and joined. Jewish visionary texts of the period describe both. Either way it puts angelic beings in a place that belongs to the Messiah.\f*, entering into things he has seen \add [i.e. going on at length about his visions]\add*, puffed up for nothing by the mind of his flesh
\v 19 and not holding fast to the Head \add [— and that is the whole of what is wrong with him]\add*, from whom the whole body, supplied and knit together through its joints and ligaments, grows with the growth that is from God.
\s1 Rules That Look Like Wisdom and Do Nothing
\p
\v 20 If you died with the Christ away from the elemental things of the world, why, as though you were living in the world \add [i.e. as though you still lived under that old jurisdiction]\add*, do you submit to decrees:
\v 21 "Do not handle, do not taste, do not touch" \add [— their rules, not mine]\add* —
\v 22 all of which are meant to perish as they are used up \add [i.e. food and drink: eaten, digested, gone]\add* — according to the commands and teachings of men?\f + \fr 2:22 \ft The wording is Isaiah's, against a religion of correct forms: "in vain do they worship me, teaching as doctrines the commandments of men" (Isaiah 29:13 LXX).\f*
\v 23 These things have a name for wisdom, with their worship of one's own choosing\f + \fr 2:23 \ft Greek \tl ethelothrēskia\tl*, a coined word: a religion of one's own devising, chosen rather than commanded.\f* and lowliness of mind and no sparing of the body, but they are of no value against the gorging of the flesh \add [i.e. they do nothing whatever to the appetite they claim to master]\add*.

\c 3
\s1 You Were Raised with Him — So Look Where Your Life Actually Is
\p
\v 1 If then you were raised together with the Christ, seek the things above, where the Christ is, seated at the right hand of God\f + \fr 3:1 \ft Psalm 110:1, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet" — the most quoted text in the earliest assemblies. The right hand is the seat from which rule is exercised. The powers the Colossian teaching busies itself with are below it.\f*.
\v 2 Set your minds on the things above, not on the things upon the earth.
\v 3 For you died, and your life has been hidden with the Christ in God \add [i.e. stored out of sight, where nothing can get at it]\add*.
\v 4 When the Christ — your life — is brought into the open \add [i.e. made visible at last, in plain view]\add*, then you also will be brought into the open with him in glory.
\s1 Put the Old Self to Death and Strip It Off
\p
\v 5 So put to death the parts of you that are upon the earth \add [i.e. everything still in you that belongs to the old life]\add*: sexual immorality, uncleanness, passion, evil craving, and greed \add [i.e. the appetite that must always have more]\add*, which is idol-worship \add [— it gives to getting the devotion that belongs to God]\add*.
\v 6 Because of these things the wrath of God is coming upon the sons of disobedience\f + \fr 3:6 \ft A Hebrew turn of phrase: "sons of" a thing means men characterized by it. These are men whose settled character is refusal.\f*.
\v 7 In these you too once walked, when you were living in them.
\v 8 But now you also put them all off \add [— off, like filthy clothing]\add*: anger, rage, malice, slander, shameful talk out of your mouth.
\v 9 Do not lie to one another, since you have stripped off the old man \add [i.e. the whole person you used to be]\add* with his practices
\v 10 and have put on the new man, the one being made new again into full knowing, after the image of the one who created him\f + \fr 3:10 \ft "Let us make man according to our image" (Genesis 1:26-27 LXX). The remaking runs backwards toward what humanity was made to be in the first place — and 1:15 has already said who bears that image perfectly.\f* —
\v 11 where there is no Greek and Jew, no circumcision and uncircumcision, no barbarian, Scythian\f + \fr 3:11 \ft A "barbarian" was anyone whose speech was not Greek — the syllables sounded like \tl bar-bar\tl* to Greek ears. Scythians, the nomads of the steppes north of the Black Sea, were the ancient world's byword for the most savage people there were. The list runs past the Jew-Gentile line and on through the ranking the Greek world used on everyone else.\f*, slave, free, but the Christ is all, and in all.
\s1 Put On What the New Self Wears
\p
\v 12 So put on, as God's chosen ones\f + \fr 3:12 \ft Greek \tl eklektoi\tl*, Israel's own word for itself: "my chosen people" (Isaiah 43:20), "Israel my chosen one" (Isaiah 45:4). It is applied here to Gentiles in a Phrygian town.\f*, holy and loved: inward parts of compassion \add [i.e. the gut-level ache you feel for someone]\add*, kindness, lowliness of mind \add [— the real thing, not the display those men make of it]\add*, gentleness, long-suffering,
\v 13 bearing with one another and freely forgiving one another if anyone has a complaint against anyone \add [— and the complaints are real ones; he is not telling you to pretend]\add*. As the Lord freely forgave you, so also you.
\v 14 And over all these, love, which is the ligament of completeness \add [i.e. what ties the whole body together and finishes it]\add*.
\v 15 And let the peace of the Christ be the umpire in your hearts \add [i.e. let it be what settles the matter when you are at odds]\add*, the peace into which you were called in one body. And become thankful people.
\v 16 Let the word of the Christ make its home in you richly, teaching and warning one another \add [— one another: this is not something done to you from outside]\add* in all wisdom, singing psalms, hymns, spiritual songs, with thankfulness in your hearts to God.
\v 17 And whatever you do, in word or in work, do all of it in the name of the Lord Jesus \add [i.e. as his authorized agent, doing it as he would do it]\add*, giving thanks to God the Father through him.
\s1 The Household, Reordered Under the Lord
\p
\v 18 Wives, place yourselves under\f + \fr 3:18 \ft The verb is military in origin — to take one's place in an ordered rank — and it stands here in the form a person uses of himself: not "be put under" but "place yourself." What follows sets the limit: what is fitting is measured "in the Lord," so the Lord, not the husband, fixes the standard.\f* your husbands, as is fitting in the Lord.
\v 19 Husbands, love your wives, and do not turn bitter toward them \add [— you hold the power in this house; you do not get to hold it harshly]\add*.
\v 20 Children, obey your parents in everything, for this is well-pleasing in the Lord.
\v 21 Fathers, do not provoke your children, so that they do not lose heart \add [i.e. so the spirit is not worn out of them]\add*.
\v 22 Slaves, obey in everything those who are your masters according to the flesh \add [i.e. the ones who own you in this present order]\add*, not with eye-service \add [i.e. working while he watches and stopping when he turns away]\add*, as people-pleasers, but with a single heart, fearing the Lord.
\v 23 Whatever you do, work at it with your whole self, as for the Lord and not for men,
\v 24 knowing that from the Lord you will receive back the inheritance as your wages\f + \fr 3:24 \ft Under Roman law a slave was property and could inherit nothing. The word used is the one from 1:12 for the allotted portion of God's people. The thing his world made legally impossible is named here as what he is owed.\f*. You are serving the Lord Christ.
\v 25 For the one who does wrong will get back the wrong he did, and there is no favoring of faces\f + \fr 3:25 \ft A Hebrew idiom — "lifting the face," showing partiality. "You shall not be partial to the poor or defer to the great" (Leviticus 19:15); God "shows no partiality and takes no bribe" (Deuteronomy 10:17). Said at the seam between slaves and masters, it cuts toward the one with the power.\f*.

\c 4
\p
\v 1 Masters, give your slaves what is right and what is equal, knowing that you also have a Master in heaven \add [i.e. you are a master, and you have one]\add*.
\s1 Pray, and Pray for Us
\p
\v 2 Hold fast to prayer, staying awake in it \add [i.e. alert, the way a man on watch stays awake]\add* with thanksgiving,
\v 3 praying at the same time for us also, that God may open to us a door for the word \add [i.e. an opening, a chance to speak]\add*, to speak the secret of the Christ — for which I am also in chains \add [— it is the message that put me here]\add* —
\v 4 that I may bring it into the open, as I must speak it.
\s1 How to Deal with Those Outside
\p
\v 5 Walk in wisdom toward those outside \add [i.e. everyone who is not part of this community]\add*, buying up the moment \add [i.e. seizing each opening while it is there to take]\add*.
\v 6 Let your word always be gracious, seasoned with salt\f + \fr 4:6 \ft Salt gave food flavor and kept it from going rotten, and no offering was to be brought without it — "the salt of the covenant" (Leviticus 2:13; Numbers 18:19). Speech with salt in it has taste and keeps.\f*, so that you know how you must answer each one \add [— each one: different people need different answers]\add*.
\s1 The Men Carrying This Letter
\p
\v 7 Everything about me Tychicus will make known to you \add [i.e. the news of me is being carried by mouth, not written here]\add*, the beloved brother and faithful servant and fellow slave in the Lord,
\v 8 whom I sent to you for this very thing, that you may know how things are with us and that he may strengthen your hearts,
\v 9 together with Onesimus\f + \fr 4:9 \ft Almost certainly the runaway slave of Paul's letter to Philemon, who belonged to a household in this town. The words used of him there — receive him "as a beloved brother" (Philemon 16) — are the words used of him here, now said out loud in front of the whole assembly he is walking back into.\f*, the faithful and beloved brother, who is one of you. They will make known to you everything here.
\s1 Greetings from Those Here with Me
\p
\v 10 Aristarchus, my fellow captive, greets you, and Mark the cousin of Barnabas — about whom you received instructions: if he comes to you, welcome him\f + \fr 4:10 \ft Mark had abandoned an earlier journey, and the quarrel over whether to take him again was sharp enough to split Paul and Barnabas and send them separate ways (Acts 15:37-40). He is here at Paul's side, with Paul's own word that he is to be received.\f* —
\v 11 and Jesus who is called Justus. These are the only ones of the circumcision \add [i.e. the only Jewish believers]\add* among my fellow workers for the kingdom of God, and they have been a comfort to me.
\v 12 Epaphras greets you, who is one of you, a slave of the Christ Jesus, always contending for you in his prayers \add [— the same word for the fight I said I was having for you; his battlefield is prayer]\add*, that you may stand complete and fully convinced in everything God wills.
\v 13 For I bear witness for him that he has much toil for you and for those in Laodicea and those in Hierapolis.
\v 14 Luke the beloved physician greets you, and Demas.
\s1 Pass This On, and a Word for Archippus
\p
\v 15 Greet the brothers in Laodicea, and Nympha and the assembly in her house\f + \fr 4:15 \ft These communities met in private homes; there were no buildings for it (Romans 16:5; Philemon 2). To host one took standing and room enough. The manuscripts that read "her house" make Nympha a woman.\f*.
\v 16 And when this letter has been read out \add [i.e. read aloud to the gathered assembly, which is how they would receive it]\add* among you, see that it is read out also in the assembly of the Laodiceans, and that you also read out the one from Laodicea\f + \fr 4:16 \ft What that letter was cannot be settled. No letter has come down to us under that name, and whether it was a lost letter of Paul's or one we know by another title is guesswork. What the instruction does show is that his letters were passed from assembly to assembly rather than kept by the one they were addressed to.\f*.
\v 17 And say to Archippus: "Watch the service you received in the Lord, so that you fill it full." \add [— said to all of you, so that all of you hold him to it]\add*
\s1 Written in My Own Hand
\p
\v 18 The greeting is in my own hand — Paul's\f + \fr 4:18 \ft The body of the letter was written by a secretary at Paul's dictation. Here he takes the pen himself, as he does elsewhere to authenticate a letter (1 Corinthians 16:21; Galatians 6:11; 2 Thessalonians 3:17).\f*. Remember my chains. Favor be with you.
